Assembler Version 03
====================
Assembled by: Version 02

Features Supported by This Assembler
-------------------------------------
This version is a clean rewrite of version 02 in version 02's own mnemonic
syntax. It supports the same feature set as version 02: non-standard 6502
mnemonics, labels, DATA directive, and operand types. The main improvement
is that the source code is now human-readable assembly rather than raw hex
DATA bytes.

Instruction mnemonics (same set as version 02):
  ADC# ADCZ ASLA BCC BCS BEQ BNE BRK CLC CMPZ,X CMP# CMP,Y DEY INCZ INX
  INY JMP JSR LDAZ LDA# LDA(),Y LDA,Y LDX# LDY# ORAZ RTS SBC# SEC STA
  STA(),Y STAZ STAZ,X TYA

DATA directive:
  DATA $XX ...        - emit hex bytes
  DATA "string"       - emit ASCII string literals with \n and \\ escapes
  DATA $XXXX          - emit 16-bit value (little-endian)
  Mixed: DATA $XX "string" $XXXX on same line

Operand types in instruction arguments:
  $XX                 - 8-bit hex value
  $XXXX               - 16-bit hex value (little-endian)
  "string"            - ASCII string with \n and \\ escape sequences
  label               - 16-bit label reference (little-endian)

Labels:
  - Defined at column 0 (no leading whitespace), bound to program counter
  - Linear search through label table at $3000
  - Forward references NOT supported (single-pass)
  - Duplicate label detection

Error handling:
  - Error 01: Duplicate label
  - Error 02: Opcode not found
  - Error 03: Label not found

Semicolon comments (to end of line).
Output starts at $2000 (hardcoded).

New in this version (relative to version 02):
- No new assembler features. This version has identical capabilities to
  version 02. The value is in the source code migration: the assembler is
  now written in its own mnemonic syntax rather than raw hex bytes,
  making it maintainable and extensible for future versions.

Features Used in This Assembler's Source Code
----------------------------------------------
The source uses version 02's mnemonic syntax throughout, replacing the hex
DATA bytes of version 02's source. This is the first version whose
source code is human-readable 6502 assembly.

Source features used:
  - Non-standard instruction mnemonics (from version 02's table)
  - Labels at column 0, bound to current program counter
  - Label references as 16-bit operands in instructions and DATA
  - DATA directive with hex bytes and string literals
  - Hex operands ($XX and $XXXX)
  - String operands ("string") with \n and \\ escapes
  - Semicolon comments
  - Branch targets as hardcoded hex offsets (e.g., BNE $06, BEQ $03)

New syntax used in source (relative to version 02):
- Non-standard instruction mnemonics (LDAZ, LDA#, STAZ, CMP#, etc.)
  instead of raw hex DATA bytes
- Whole label names (02's source, written for 01, has names that differ
  in their first character)
- Label references in instruction operands (e.g., JSR read, JMP lnloop)
  instead of DATA (02's source has DATA $20 r_read)
